==In animation==
[[File:Brock doctor outfit.png|thumb|220px|Brock, a Doctor-in-training]]
[[File:Pokémon Doctor class.png|thumb|left|220px|A Pokémon Doctor's class]]
Unlike their real world counterparts, Pokémon Doctors do not appear to be preferred over [[Nurse Joy]] and the healing capabilities of [[Pokémon Center]]s. This seems to be evidenced by all of the doctors in {{aniseries|PTS}} operating on their own or in small offices located some distance from Pokémon Centers. Doctors have a wide range of knowledge, being able to treat many symptoms and knowing what types of {{Berries}} and other healing items to use. Some doctors are also experts with [[Evolution]].

==={{aniseries|RS}}===
In ''[[AG015|Gonna Rule The School!]]'', a Pokémon Doctor's class was taught at the [[Pokémon Trainers' School]] in [[Rustboro City]].

In ''[[AG060|Delcatty Got Your Tongue]]'', {{Ash}} and {{ashfr}} visited a Pokémon clinic located in [[Foothill Town]]. It is run by [[Dr. Abby]], a [[Top Coordinator]] who decided to learn about natural Pokémon medicine after her {{p|Skitty}} was badly injured in a {{pkmn|battle}}.

==={{aniseries|DP}}===
In ''[[DP190|The Brockster Is In!]]'', Nurse Joy suggested to {{an|Brock}} that he become a Pokémon Doctor after witnessing him treat a group of {{status|poison}}ed Pokémon. In [[DP191|the next episode]], Brock revealed that he would follow her advice and study to become a Doctor.

Notably, Pokémon Doctors have been present in the {{pkmn|animated series}} long before the Trainer class was first introduced in the games.

